id: tail-attack
name: Tail Attack
type: instant
actionCost: 0
kiCostPerTier: 2
attacking: true
requiresTarget: true
special: true
usageLimit: 1/round
profile: any
tailAttack: true
outsideDiminishing: true
tags: unarmed
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Strike with your tail as an Instant. An attack that costs no Action, sits outside
  Diminishing Offense in both directions, and uses the Simple Profile - or the one other
  Profile your tail was built for, for more Ki.
text: >
  Tail Attack Maneuver [1/Round]: Strike out at an opponent using your tail.
  –Maneuver Type: Instant Maneuver
  –Action Cost: N/A
  –Minions: Free
  –KP Cost: 2(T)
  –Effects: Target an Opponent with an Unarmed Attacking Maneuver using the Simple
  Profile. When you first gain access to this Special Maneuver, you may select one of
  these additional effects to have access to while you have access to this Maneuver:

  * Elongated. You may increase the KP Cost of this Maneuver by 2(T) to instead use the
    Sweeping Profile.
  * Multiple. You may increase the KP Cost of this Maneuver by 2(T) to instead use the
    Combination Profile of the Physical Foundation.
  * Spiked. You may increase the KP Cost of this Maneuver by 4(T) to instead use the
    Crushing Profile.
  * Heavy. You may increase the KP Cost of this Maneuver by 6(T) to instead use the
    Powered Profile.

  Attacking Maneuvers made through the Tail Attack Maneuver do not suffer from, or count
  towards, the penalty from Diminishing Offense.

---

# --- The first Special Maneuver that is itself an attack ----------------------------------
#
# Everything Special before it either marked somebody, opened a Clash, or handed an attack
# over for somebody to declare afterwards. This one is the attack: `attacking` is on it, so
# it goes down the road a Basic Attack goes down - Profile and Foundation declared, the
# price settled by the Profile, Strike against a defence, Wound less Soak.
#
# An Instant, which is what makes it worth having. No Action, only in answer to a Standard
# Maneuver, and never two in a row - all three of which live in the Instant rule already
# and needed nothing here.
#
# --- What it costs, which is the one reading worth arguing about --------------------------
#
# "KP Cost: 2(T)", and each variant "increases the KP Cost of this Maneuver" by 2(T), 2(T),
# 4(T) or 6(T).
#
# Built the way this system prices every other attack: what the Maneuver states and what
# the Profile states are added. That is what `maneuverKiCost` has always done, and this
# entry never says the Profile is free - so a Tail Attack on the Sweeping Profile is 2(T)
# for the Maneuver, 2(T) for the variant and 4(T) for Sweeping itself.
#
# The other reading is written down here because the numbers point at it hard. Take the
# Maneuver's 2(T) as the whole price and add only the variant:
#
#     Simple       2(T)                 Simple's own cost is 0
#     Sweeping     2 + 2 = 4(T)         Sweeping's own cost is 4(T)
#     Combination  2 + 2 = 4(T)         Combination's own cost is 3(T)
#     Crushing     2 + 4 = 6(T)         Crushing's own cost is 6(T)
#     Powered      2 + 6 = 8(T)         Powered's own cost is 8(T)
#
# Three of the four land exactly on the Profile's own listed cost, which is the number you
# get if the variant amounts were worked out as "what the Profile costs, less the 2(T)
# already paid". That is a strong hint that the intended total is the Profile's price and
# no more. It is a hint rather than a rule, so it is not what is built, and Combination
# misses it by one either way.
#
# If the table wants the other reading it is one line - the surcharge stands and the
# Profile's own cost comes off. Nothing else in the build depends on which it is.
#
# The Minimum Ki Point Cost is untouched by the surcharge, which is right either way: that
# floor is half the Profile's *listed* cost, and a surcharge is this Maneuver's own price
# rising rather than the Profile's.
#
# --- The choice made once -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "When you first gain access to this Special Maneuver, you may select one of these
# additional effects to have access to while you have access to this Maneuver."
#
# One of four, or none - "you may select" - and then kept. So it is stored on the Item,
# which is the thing here that remembers what a player did to their own copy of a Maneuver:
# the same place a rename lives, and it survives the Maneuver going off the list and coming
# back.
#
# "When you first gain access" is a moment this system has no hook for. Access is a
# question asked fresh every time anything wants the answer - a Skill's Ranks, or a Slot an
# effect wrote - and nothing fires when the answer changes. So the choice is asked at the
# first use instead, which is the first moment there is anybody to ask it of, and it is
# editable on the Maneuver's own sheet afterwards like everything else about the Item.
#
# Blank means it has not been asked yet; "none" means it was asked and declined. Two states
# rather than one, because a player who took nothing must not be asked again every round.
#
# --- Which Foundation ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The entry pins one, once: "the Combination Profile of the Physical Foundation." Built
# exactly there and nowhere else.
#
# Sweeping, Crushing and Powered are Physical Profiles already, so they need no pinning.
# Simple is not - it belongs to all three Foundations - and the entry says nothing about
# it.
#
# Settled by the table: the Simple Profile may be made with any Foundation, and is asked
# which the way every other Simple attack is asked. So a Tail Attack is pinned to Physical
# in exactly the one place the entry pins it and nowhere else.
#
# Which was the open question here, and the reading it went against is worth keeping: a
# tail is a limb, so all of this being Physical is the obvious guess. Against that, the
# entry had its chance to say so in the one place it was needed and did not, and pinning a
# Foundation the text leaves open takes an option off a player. The table agreed, and this
# is no longer a guess either way.
#
# --- Outside Diminishing Offense, both ways -----------------------------------------------
#
# "Attacking Maneuvers made through the Tail Attack Maneuver do not suffer from, or count
# towards, the penalty from Diminishing Offense."
#
# One sentence, two pieces of machinery, so it is built as two:
#
#   - "do not suffer from" is the Strike Roll. The Dim. Offense row is left off the
#     breakdown entirely rather than shown at nothing: a row saying it took nothing off is
#     a row a reader has to work out the meaning of.
#   - "do not count towards" is the round's attack count, which every other attack raises
#     as it is declared. This one leaves it alone, so the attack after it is no worse off
#     for this one having happened.
#
# The second half is the one that would have gone unnoticed. Diminishing Offense is worked
# out from a count on the character, and a count is easy to read and easy to forget not to
# write.
#
# --- "Unarmed" ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Carried as a tag, because a tag is what these rules match on - "any Maneuver tagged
# signature" is how Transfigured refuses one. Nothing reads `unarmed` yet: there are no
# weapons in this system, so there is nothing for the word to be the opposite of. It is
# recorded because the entry says it, and the day something asks for an Unarmed Attacking
# Maneuver the answer is already on the Maneuver.
#
# --- What is not built --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Minions: Free." There are no Minions in this system, which the Arrogant Declaration,
# Analysis, Hype, Brace, Flip, Holding Back and Sense files all say about their own Minion
# lines.
#
# Having a tail. Nothing here knows whether a character has one - there are no Racial Trait
# files in the library and no body parts anywhere - so what opens this is an effect
# granting it, like every Special Maneuver no Skill opens. The Tail Restraint rule is on
# the same list of things waiting for a tail to exist.
#
# --- How it is gained ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Not known. No Skill's entry names it, and four variants chosen once read more like a
# racial or a Talent choice than like something Ranks hand over - "when you first gain
# access" is written as though access arrives once and stays. Until an entry says so, it is
# one of the Special Maneuvers an effect has to grant.
